playerOrders.description:
Player Orders is an enterprise order-management platform for digital game products built with SAP CAP, TypeScript, SAPUI5, OData V4 and SAP HANA Cloud. I designed and implemented the domain model, service layer, Fiori interface, authorization model and integrations across the complete order lifecycle.

Order lifecycle and inventory:
- implemented controlled transitions from NEW through SUBMITTED, PAID and FULFILLED, with validated cancellation and issue-reporting paths;
- created sales orders, line items, payment transactions and fulfillment logs with transactional processing;
- added automatic line totals, order validation, stock checks, replenishment and calculated inventory statuses;
- built product management with activation, pricing, image upload and stock controls;
- preserved payment and fulfillment history as auditable business records.

Security and service architecture:
- implemented Customer, SalesAdmin, WarehouseManager and system-user roles with SAP XSUAA;
- enforced customer-level row isolation in the CAP service so users can access only their own orders;
- exposed OData actions for submission, payment, fulfillment, cancellation, issue reporting and replenishment;
- integrated Player CRM profiles and created a protected system-to-system order-summary API;
- prepared the solution for SAP BTP with HANA Cloud, HDI, Approuter and HTML5 Application Repository services.

SAPUI5 interface and quality:
- built responsive order and product workspaces with search, filters, sorting, detail pages and role-aware actions;
- created localized dialogs for orders, payments, fulfillment, stock, images and support cases;
- added English and Russian localization plus desktop side navigation and mobile bottom navigation;
- wrote integration tests for authorization, lifecycle transitions, inventory, media handling and service integrations.

The project demonstrates an enterprise SAP application where UI capabilities, backend authorization, transactional business rules and cross-service integration operate as one coherent system.

playerCrm.description:
Player CRM is an SAP-based customer relationship management application for player profiles, activity, preferences, loyalty programs, feedback and marketing data. I built the project with SAP CAP, CDS, OData V4 and SAP Fiori Elements, keeping the domain, service and metadata-driven UI layers clearly separated.

CRM domain and workflows:
- modeled players, statuses, preferences, interactions, feedback, loyalty programs and marketing campaigns with associations and compositions;
- created list-report and object-page experiences with profile, interaction, feedback and loyalty sections;
- added filtering, value helps, draft-enabled editing and related-data navigation;
- implemented automatic average-rating recalculation after feedback is created;
- assigned AtRisk status automatically when a low rating indicates a retention risk.

VIP and business logic:
- implemented a custom activateVIP CAP action for 7-, 30- and 90-day membership periods;
- calculated pricing and membership dates, created loyalty records and updated the player profile in the service layer;
- used managed fields, validation rules and seed data to support realistic enterprise workflows;
- prepared the campaign model for segmentation, targeted communication and retention scenarios.

SAP implementation and testing:
- exposed the CRM domain through OData V4 projections with CAP draft support;
- generated the Fiori Elements interface through UI annotations, selection fields, facets and header metadata;
- supported SQLite for local development and SAP HANA for deployment;
- added OPA5 integration journeys for filters, table columns, navigation and object-page sections.

The result is an extensible SAP CRM foundation that combines declarative Fiori UI, service-side business rules and a domain model prepared for further analytics, segmentation and authorization features.

pulse.description:
Pulse is a frontend prototype of a European fintech super app that combines personal banking, business finance, investing, mobile connectivity, insurance, travel and marketplace services in one digital ecosystem. I designed and implemented the React and TypeScript frontend as a complete product experience: from the public website and authentication to multi-domain financial workspaces.

Pulse is intentionally frontend-only. It does not connect to real banks, payment providers or personal financial data. Balances, transfers, purchases, subscriptions, investments and other operations are simulated locally in the browser to demonstrate realistic product flows.

Product ecosystem:
- built the public Pulse website with product pages, services, news, careers, contacts, privacy content and responsive navigation;
- developed personal banking for EUR, GBP and USD accounts, cards, payments, transfers, operation history, rewards and card controls;
- created a business workspace with invoices, drafts, team members, business cards, spending limits and payment flows;
- implemented investing with portfolio management, purchases, sales, order states, cancellation and transaction history;
- developed mobile services with plans, eSIM activation and transfer, roaming packages and automatic renewal;
- built insurance policy, premium, claim, document and demonstration reimbursement flows;
- added travel destinations, trip planning and saved trips;
- created a marketplace with catalog, favorites, cart, checkout, orders, cancellation and repeat purchase;
- implemented registration, login, recovery, confirmation, protected workspaces and session restoration.

Shared financial core:
- created a typed local ledger shared by banking, business, investing, mobile, insurance, travel and marketplace domains;
- standardized transaction metadata, balance updates, service sources, operation status and history;
- added insufficient-balance protection, repeat operations, cancellation and reversible transaction support;
- implemented versioned LocalStorage persistence with schema validation and migration of older data;
- synchronized financial state between product areas through shared models and browser events.

Architecture and product quality:
- React 19, TypeScript and Vite with domain-oriented modules and route-level code splitting;
- React Router for public, authentication, banking and service-specific routes;
- Redux Toolkit for authentication and order state, with MobX for structured product content;
- i18next with complete English and Russian localization and persisted language selection;
- a unified SCSS design system using shared tokens, restrained visual effects and responsive layouts;
- accessible forms and dialogs with focus management, Escape handling, scroll locking, keyboard navigation and aria-live feedback;
- global error boundary plus consistent loading, empty, disabled, success and error states;
- responsive behavior for mobile, tablet and desktop, including bottom-sheet interactions.

Testing and delivery:
- Vitest coverage for ledger calculations, transaction application, persistence validation and migrations;
- route smoke tests across public and authenticated product areas on desktop and mobile viewports;
- production-build checks, ESLint, Prettier and optimized Vite/Rollup output.

The result is a browser-based fintech product demonstration that shows how a large multi-service frontend can behave as one coherent system while remaining explicit about its simulated financial data.

londonGrill.description:
London Grill is a bilingual restaurant web application that brings menu discovery, table reservations, food and drink pre-orders, event stories and personal visit management into one digital guest experience. I developed the React frontend, booking interactions, public pages and data flows for both a self-contained demo mode and a Firebase-backed environment.

Guest experience and content:
- built a distinctive responsive homepage with restaurant branding, promotional sections and direct calls to action;
- created searchable food and drink menus with localized categories, product cards, prices, volumes and responsive grids;
- implemented stories and event detail pages together with reusable editorial components;
- developed a responsive restaurant gallery with full-size viewing and keyboard-friendly navigation;
- added complete English and Russian localization for navigation, menu content, forms, statuses, validation and notifications.

Reservations and personal visits:
- developed an interactive SVG floor plan with available, selected, unavailable and temporarily held table states;
- implemented date and time selection, 30-minute slots, guest-count recommendations and detailed booking validation;
- added availability checks, confirmation, editing and cancellation together with clear success and error feedback;
- built the My Evening area for upcoming and previous visits, reservation details and pre-ordered items;
- created a menu pre-order flow with quantities, totals, editing, removal and persistence.

Firebase and demo modes:
- integrated Firebase Authentication for customer sessions and protected booking information;
- connected reservations, availability and pre-orders to Cloud Firestore when Firebase is configured;
- used Firestore transactions and temporary table holds to prevent conflicting bookings;
- separated anonymized public availability from private guest information;
- implemented a fully functional LocalStorage demo mode that works without backend credentials and preserves bookings between reloads;
- built role-aware administration views for reviewing reservations, pre-orders and availability, including support for Firebase admin authorization.

Architecture and quality:
- React 19, JavaScript, Vite and React Router with route-level lazy loading and Suspense fallbacks;
- feature-oriented structure separating public pages, layout, catalog data, booking logic and reusable UI;
- MobX and reusable state models for menu selection, bookings and visit management;
- CSS Modules and responsive layouts adapted for desktop, tablet and mobile;
- loading, empty, disabled, success and error states across asynchronous and form-based flows;
- automated tests for reservation behavior, validation, local workflows and Firestore security rules;
- ESLint, Prettier and production-build validation for consistent delivery.

The result is a production-style restaurant platform that covers the full journey from discovering the venue to booking a table, preparing an order and managing an upcoming evening.

vinylStore.description:
Vinyl Store is a full-stack marketplace for discovering, buying and selling vinyl records. I was responsible exclusively for the frontend: application architecture, interface implementation, responsive layouts, state management, authentication flows and integration with data services.

For the deployed demonstration, I adapted the frontend to Supabase for authentication, database operations, file storage and persistent application data. The original backend and server-side architecture were not developed by me.

Marketplace experience:
- built a responsive catalog with search, sorting, pagination and filters by artist, genre, release year, price and availability;
- created detailed release pages with artwork, seller information, inventory, ratings and reviews;
- implemented seller storefronts together with loading, empty and error states;
- added reusable product cards, responsive filters and mobile navigation.

Commerce and customer flows:
- implemented registration, login, password recovery, persistent sessions and protected routes;
- built shopping cart and wishlist flows with quantity controls, validation, totals and synchronized user data;
- created checkout, order summary and confirmation interfaces, including Stripe-related frontend integration;
- developed editable customer profiles, localized validation, notifications and confirmation dialogs.

Seller and administration tools:
- built seller dashboards for profile, inventory, listings and order management;
- implemented forms for creating and editing releases, prices, quantities and product images;
- developed role-protected admin interfaces for users, sellers, artists, genres and vinyl releases;
- added seller approval flows, management tables and safe destructive-action confirmations.

Architecture and delivery:
- React, TypeScript and Vite with a feature-oriented application structure;
- Redux Toolkit for typed global state and asynchronous data operations;
- React Router for public, protected, dynamic and role-aware routes;
- Supabase Authentication, Database and Storage for the standalone demo environment;
- i18next with English and Russian localization and persistent language selection;
- modular SCSS, reusable UI components and adaptive layouts for desktop, tablet and mobile;
- consistent handling of validation, loading, success, empty and error states.

The result is a production-style marketplace frontend that covers the complete customer journey as well as seller and administrator workflows.

pet.description:
Betera Green is an independent interactive portfolio concept focused on environmental and social impact, sustainability education, animal welfare and responsible community participation. I designed and implemented the complete React and TypeScript frontend, combining editorial content, verified impact data and lightweight interactive experiences in one responsive application.

Platform and editorial experience:
- built the main landing page, shared navigation and responsive layout for desktop, tablet and mobile;
- created an initiatives catalog with reusable cards and dynamic detail pages based on URL slugs;
- presented goals, activities and measurable results together with source references, verification dates and image credits;
- developed an impact dashboard with metrics, progress timeline, regional map content and clear methodology explanations;
- structured editorial and impact data in centralized modules so new initiatives can be added without rewriting page components.

Interactive features:
- developed Green Rush, an educational waste-sorting mini-game with keyboard controls, scoring, progress, completion and restart states;
- separated game rules from rendering through a typed reducer and predictable state transitions;
- added automated Vitest coverage for scoring, progression, restart and edge cases;
- created an animal assistance catalog, pet care content and clear calls to action;
- implemented weekly sustainability challenges with locally persisted progress.

Architecture and implementation:
- React 19, TypeScript and Vite with page-based structure and reusable layout components;
- React Router with nested routes, dynamic initiative URLs and fallback handling;
- typed models for initiatives, metrics, game state and reducer actions;
- reusable source links, animal icons, content cards and interactive game components;
- custom internationalization layer with centralized, translation-ready interface content;
- SCSS-based responsive grids, typography and interaction patterns;
- semantic HTML, descriptive alternative text and visible keyboard interaction states;
- LocalStorage persistence with safe handling of missing or invalid values.

Quality and delivery:
- TypeScript checks, ESLint with strict warnings, Vitest and production-build validation;
- lightweight client-side architecture that works without a backend;
- organized content and media metadata prepared for future API or CMS integration.

The result is a data-driven sustainability platform that combines credible presentation, accessible education and tested interactive functionality. It is an independent portfolio concept and not an official Betera product.

beats.description:
Beats Landing is a mobile-first product page built with TypeScript and pure CSS.

Beats Landing is a minimalist product landing page designed with a mobile-first approach for showcasing audio products. The project stands out with a balanced stack of TypeScript (47%) and pure CSS (51%), demonstrating the ability to build modern websites without styling frameworks, with a focus on performance and code clarity.

Implemented functionality:

Mobile-First Design Philosophy:
- responsive layout optimized primarily for smartphones;
- smooth scaling to tablet and desktop without quality loss;
- optimized touch targets for mobile devices;
- fluid typography and responsive spacing system.

Pure CSS Architecture (51%):
- no CSS-in-JS or preprocessors, pure CSS only;
- modern CSS features (CSS Grid, Flexbox, Custom Properties);
- dedicated styles/ directory for modular organization;
- CSS modules or BEM methodology usage;
- cross-browser compatibility without extra dependencies.

TypeScript Integration (47%):
- strict typing for all interactive components;
- type-safe event handlers and DOM manipulations;
- separate tsconfig.json and tsconfig.node.json configurations;
- TypeScript used for build scripts and tooling.

Product Showcase Features:
- hero section with product visualization;
- product specs and characteristics;
- image gallery with interactive elements;
- call-to-action blocks for conversion.

Modern Development Setup:
- Vite as an ultra-fast bundler;
- ESLint for code quality and consistency;
- hot module replacement for fast development;
- optimized production build with minification.

Key technical solutions:

Vanilla CSS Mastery:
- CSS Custom Properties for theming;
- CSS Grid for complex layouts;
- Flexbox for flexible components;
- media queries for responsive breakpoints;
- CSS animations without JavaScript overhead;
- progressive enhancement approach.

TypeScript Configuration:
- strict typing via tsconfig.json;
- separate config for Node.js scripts (tsconfig.node.json);
- ES module output for modern browsers;
- strict mode for maximum type safety.

Vite Build System:
- custom vite.config.ts configuration;
- asset optimization and code splitting;
- dev server with instant HMR;
- production build with tree-shaking.

Project Structure:
- clear src/ separation for source code;
- styles/ for CSS modules and global styles;
- public/ for static assets (images, fonts);
- modular architecture for scalability.

Code Quality:
- ESLint configuration for TypeScript;
- consistent code style;
- automated linting in development workflow;
- pre-commit hooks for code quality.

Performance Optimization:
- minimal JavaScript for better performance;
- CSS-only animations where possible;
- lazy loading of images;
- optimized assets via Vite;
- small bundle size due to no heavy libraries.

Responsive Images:
- adaptive images via picture element;
- optimization for different screen densities (1x, 2x);
- WebP with JPEG/PNG fallback;
- lazy loading for above-the-fold content.

Deployment:
- Vercel setup through vercel.json;
- SPA routing configuration;
- optimized production build;
- automatic deployment on push to main branch;
- environment-based configuration.

Approach advantages:

Vanilla CSS Benefits:
- zero runtime dependencies for styles;
- maximum performance;
- full styling control without abstractions;
- smaller bundle size;
- better understanding of CSS fundamentals.

TypeScript Safety:
- early error detection;
- improved developer experience with autocomplete;
- self-documenting code via types;
- safer refactoring.

Mobile-First Advantages:
- better performance on mobile devices;
- progressive enhancement for desktop;
- forced prioritization of important content;
- improved accessibility.

nike.description:
Nike Product Slider is an interactive web component that showcases a modern product slider inspired by premium brand design. The project demonstrates a strong understanding of building visually engaging user interfaces with smooth animations and responsive behavior.

Implemented functionality:
- full-screen slider using CSS viewport-relative units (100vw / 100vh);
- smooth transitions between slides with autoplay support;
- manual navigation via left/right arrow controls;
- layered typography effects (filled + outlined text) for visual depth;
- animated shadows and floating effects for product images;
- interactive rating system (stars) with fill logic up to the selected position;
- functional BUY NOW button with a dynamic cart counter;
- adaptive layout for all device sizes with dedicated breakpoints.

Key technical solutions:
- responsiveness: CSS Grid and Flexbox are used to create a flexible layout that adapts to any screen size;
- performance: Vite provides fast builds and optimized asset loading;
- UX: smooth CSS transitions and animations deliver natural interactions without lag;
- state management: JavaScript logic controls current slide, counters, and animation states.

Deployment:
 project is configured for automatic deployment to GitHub Pages via npm scripts.

energo.description:
Energy Construction is an HTML landing page with Owl Carousel and SVG iconography.

Energy Construction is a classic corporate landing page for a construction company, built on core web technologies without modern UI frameworks. The project demonstrates strong vanilla JavaScript skills, advanced CSS usage, and integration of popular jQuery plugins to deliver a professional business website.

Implemented functionality:

Owl Carousel integration:
- interactive sliders for showcasing projects and services;
- responsive carousel with touch support for mobile devices;
- autoplay with configurable intervals;
- navigation via dots and arrows;
- smooth transitions between slides;
- lazy loading of images for performance optimization.

SVG iconography system:
- scalable vector icons via SVG sprites;
- SVG fonts for consistent iconography;
- icon styling through CSS;
- reduced file size with SVG optimization;
- accessibility support via ARIA labels;
- retina-ready graphics with no quality loss.

Corporate content structure:
- hero section with compelling headline and CTA;
- About section with company story and mission;
- completed projects portfolio with gallery;
- services list with detailed descriptions;
- contact form for lead generation;
- footer with contact details and social links.

Responsive design:
- adaptive layout for all device types;
- mobile-first or desktop-first implementation;
- breakpoints for phones, tablets, and desktop;
- optimized images for different resolutions.

Key technical solutions:

HTML semantic markup (43.6%):
